<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#spice-trade">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spice trade</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as spice monopolies, spices</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The spice trade was the long-distance commerce in spices such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> from South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. It enriched intermediaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Empires including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> empires, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> profited from controlling it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spice-trade.jpg" alt="Spice trade" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: judepics · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
